You are reverse-engineering a compiled C++ binary as a BLACK BOX. The binary is at /workspace/dataset/test15_executable.

Invocation contract: it takes EXACTLY 6 positional args: scheme host port path query fragment.
It prints 8 lines to stdout: scheme(), host(), port(), path(), query(), fragment(), to_string(), is_valid().

RULES OF ENGAGEMENT:
- You may ONLY run the binary and observe stdout/stderr/exit code. There is NO C++ source available; do not look for any.
- ALWAYS quote every argument in the shell, including empty ones: /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "http" "example.com" "80" "/" "" ""
- Make output unambiguous. Use a helper that numbers the lines and marks empties, e.g.:
    p(){ printf '### IN: [%s]\n' "$*"; /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "$@" >/tmp/o 2>/tmp/e; printf '  exit=%d\n' $?; nl -ba -s': ' /tmp/o | sed 's/$/<END>/'; if [ -s /tmp/e ]; then echo "  STDERR: $(cat /tmp/e)"; fi; }
  Then call p with 6 quoted args. Beware: trailing empty args are easy to lose — verify your helper really passes 6.
- Run MANY cases (30+ minimum). Batch them into a few Bash calls; do not do one call per case.
- Be exhaustive about the boundary cases in your assigned dimension.
- Do not guess. Every rule you report must be backed by an observed input/output pair you actually ran.

Baseline facts already established (re-verify if relevant to you):
- ("http","example.com","80","/","","")  -> lines: http / example.com / 80 / "/" / "" / "" / "http://example.com:80/" / 1
- ("http","example.com","-1","/","","") -> to_string is "http://example.com/"  (port omitted), port() prints -1
- ("http","example.com","0","/","","")  -> to_string is "http://example.com:0/"  (port 0 IS included)
- ("http","","80","/","","")            -> to_string is "http://:80/", is_valid=1
- ("","example.com","80","/","","")     -> to_string is "/" (scheme+authority both vanish), is_valid=1
- ("","","0","","","")                  -> to_string is "", is_valid=0

Report rules as precise, implementable statements (pseudocode-level), each with the concrete evidence line.


DIMENSION UNDER REVIEW: harness-argc-and-io

A previous investigator probed this dimension and inferred the following rules. Your job is ADVERSARIAL: actively try to FALSIFY each rule by finding inputs where it predicts the wrong output. For every rule, design at least 2 new inputs (not already tested) that would expose the rule as wrong if it is wrong, run them, and compare prediction vs reality. Pay special attention to off-by-one boundaries, empty strings, sign boundaries, and whether a rule that was only tested with one other field set still holds when that field is empty.

RULES TO ATTACK:
[
  {
    "rule": "The wrapper requires at least 6 positional args (argc >= 7). With 0,1,2,3,4, or 5 positional args it exits immediately with status 1, writing ZERO bytes to stdout AND ZERO bytes to stderr (silent failure, no usage message). rc<128 => normal exit(1), not a signal. Pseudocode: if (argc < 7) return 1;",
    "evidence": "argv=() -> exit=1, stdout 0 bytes, stderr 0 bytes; (\"http\") -> exit=1 0B/0B; (\"http\",\"example.com\") -> exit=1 0B/0B; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\") -> exit=1 0B/0B; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\") -> exit=1 0B/0B; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\") -> exit=1 0B/0B. Compact table: n=0 rc=1 stdout=0B stderr=0B / n=3 rc=1 stdout=0B stderr=0B / n=5 rc=1 stdout=0B stderr=0B. Also with all-non-empty args: (\"a\"),(\"a\",\"b\"),(\"a\",\"b\",\"80\"),(\"a\",\"b\",\"80\",\"d\"),(\"a\",\"b\",\"80\",\"d\",\"e\") all -> exit=1, stdout EMPTY."
  },
  {
    "rule": "Exactly argv[1..6] are consumed. Any extra args (7th onward) are silently ignored and produce byte-identical output; nothing from argv[7+] ever leaks into stdout.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"e.com\",\"80\",\"/p\",\"q\",\"f\") -> cksum \"1771325740 45\"; same + \"Z\" -> \"1771325740 45\"; same + \"Z\",\"Y\",\"X\",\"W\",\"V\",\"U\" (12 args) -> \"1771325740 45\"; same + a..n (20 args) -> \"1771325740 45\". Also (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\",\"EXTRA7\",\"EXTRA8\") -> identical 8 lines ending \"http://example.com:80/\" / \"1\"; and a 26-arg call (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/p\",\"q=1\",\"f\",1..20) -> line7=\"http://example.com:80/p?q=1#f\"."
  },
  {
    "rule": "On success (argc>=7 and port parseable) stdout is exactly 8 records in this fixed order: 1 scheme(), 2 host(), 3 port(), 4 path(), 5 query(), 6 fragment(), 7 to_string(), 8 is_valid(). Exit status is 0 and stderr is empty.",
    "evidence": "(\"SCHEME1\",\"HOST2\",\"3\",\"PATH4\",\"QUERY5\",\"FRAG6\") -> 1:scheme1 2:HOST2 3:3 4:/PATH4 5:QUERY5 6:FRAG6 7:scheme1://HOST2:3/PATH4?QUERY5#FRAG6 8:1, exit=0, stderr empty. Each of the 6 distinct sentinel values appears on exactly its own numbered line."
  },
  {
    "rule": "Each of the 8 records is terminated by exactly one LF (0x0A). There is no CR anywhere, no CRLF, no blank 9th line, and no extra trailing newline beyond line 8's. Total stdout bytes == sum(len(field_i) for i=1..8) + 8. The last byte of stdout is always 0x0a.",
    "evidence": "od -c of (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\"): `h t t p \\n e x a m p l e . c o m \\n 8 0 \\n / \\n \\n \\n h t t p : / / e x a m p l e . c o m : 8 0 / \\n 1 \\n` = 49 bytes total; newline_count=8, CR_count=0, last byte hex = 0a. Field lengths 4+11+2+1+0+0+22+1=41, +8 newlines = 49. Second case (\"https\",\"h.io\",\"443\",\"/a/b\",\"k=v&z=1\",\"frag\") -> od shows ...`a g \\n 1 \\n`, total=69 nl=8 cr=0. Third case (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> od -c = `\\n \\n 0 \\n \\n \\n \\n \\n 0 \\n`, total=10 nl=8. Invariant sweep of 20 further inputs: every one gave nl=8, cr=0, last=0a."
  },
  {
    "rule": "Each record is flushed individually — the program issues 8 separate write() calls (one per line), i.e. it uses std::endl (or an explicit flush) after every field, not a bare \"\\n\". Consequence: a consumer reading the pipe sees line-aligned chunks, and any output produced before a crash is already on the fd.",
    "evidence": "Reading the binary's stdout through a pipe with sysread: (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") returned 3 reads: 22B \"http\\nexample.com\\n80\\n/\\n\", 2B \"\\n\\n\", 25B \"http://example.com:80/\\n1\\n\" — more than one write for a 49-byte total, which full buffering would have emitted as a single 49B write. Over 300 iterations of (\"AAAA\",\"BBBBBBBB\",\"12345\",\"/CCCCCCCCCCCC\",\"DDDDDD\",\"EEE\"): distinct internal write-boundary offsets observed = {5,14,20,34,41,45,91}, which are EXACTLY the cumulative offsets after lines 1-7 (line lens 4,8,5,13,6,3,45 -> cum 5,14,20,34,41,45,91), and \"ALL observed write boundaries fall exactly after a '\\n' (line-aligned)\" with zero misaligned boundaries in 300 runs. Same result for the baseline case: boundaries {17,20,22,23,24,47}, all line-aligned."
  },
  {
    "rule": "is_valid() is printed as an int-style bool: literally the single byte '1' or '0' followed by LF. Never \"true\"/\"false\" (no std::boolalpha), regardless of locale.",
    "evidence": "line 8 of (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"\") piped to od -c -> `0 \\n` (2 bytes). line 8 of (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> `1 \\n` (2 bytes). grep -ciE 'true|false' over full output for ports 80,-1,0,65535 -> 0 matches each. Across 48 combinations of scheme/host/port/path the only line-8 values were: 12x \"0\", 36x \"1\". LC_ALL=C / en_US.UTF-8 / de_DE.UTF-8 all -> line8=[0] for (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"\")."
  },
  {
    "rule": "port() is printed with plain operator<<(std::ostream&,int): base-10, no zero padding, no field width, no '+' for positives, no leading/trailing spaces, no thousands separators. A negative port renders as '-' followed by digits.",
    "evidence": "od -c of line 3: port arg \"0\" -> `0 \\n`; \"-1\" -> `- 1 \\n`; \"65535\" -> `6 5 5 3 5 \\n`; \"7\" -> `7 \\n`. Width sweep: 1->[1], 9->[9], 10->[10], 99->[99], 100->[100], 999->[999], 1000->[1000], 9999->[9999], 10000->[10000], 65535->[65535]. Input \"+80\" -> line3 \"80\" (no plus retained); \"080\" -> \"80\"; \"00000080\" -> \"80\" (no padding retained)."
  },
  {
    "rule": "port() can only ever print an integer in [-1, 65535]: any parsed int outside [0,65535] is normalized to -1 before storage, so INT_MIN/INT_MAX never appear on line 3. Pseudocode: stored_port = (p >= 0 && p <= 65535) ? p : -1;",
    "evidence": "port arg \"2147483647\" (INT_MAX) -> line3 \"-1\", to_string \"http://example.com/\"; \"-2147483648\" (INT_MIN) -> line3 \"-1\", to_string \"http://example.com/\"; \"65536\" -> \"-1\"; \"65537\" -> \"-1\"; \"70000\" -> \"-1\"; \"100000\" -> \"-1\"; \"1000000\" -> \"-1\"; \"2147483646\" -> \"-1\"; \"999999\" -> \"-1\"; \"-2\" -> \"-1\"; \"-5\" -> \"-1\". In-range preserved verbatim: -1->-1, 0->0, 1->1, 79->79, 80->80, 1023->1023, 1024->1024, 8080->8080, 65534->65534, 65535->65535."
  },
  {
    "rule": "argv[3] is converted with std::stoi (base 10) and the exception is NOT caught: leading whitespace is skipped, an optional +/- sign is accepted, parsing stops at the first non-digit and trailing garbage is ignored. An empty or wholly non-numeric argv[3] throws std::invalid_argument; a value outside int range throws std::out_of_range. Either throw reaches terminate -> SIGABRT, exit code 134, ZERO bytes on stdout, and a fixed 87-byte libstdc++ message on stderr.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com\",\" 80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line3 \"80\" (leading space skipped). (\"...\",\"80 \",...) -> \"80\". (\"...\",\"80abc\",...) -> \"80\" (trailing garbage ignored). (\"...\",\"0x10\",...) -> \"0\" (base 10, stops at 'x'). (\"...\",\"\",...) -> exit=134, stdout 0 bytes, stderr \"terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'|  what():  stoi|\". (\"...\",\"abc\",...) -> exit=134 same invalid_argument. (\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\",\"e\",\"f\") -> exit=134 invalid_argument/stoi. (\"...\",\"2147483648\",...) -> exit=134 stderr \"...std::out_of_range...what():  stoi\"; \"-2147483649\" -> out_of_range; \"99999999999999999999\" -> out_of_range; \"-99999999999999999999\" -> out_of_range. All abort cases: stderr_bytes=87. rc=134 = 128+SIGABRT(6)."
  },
  {
    "rule": "The port conversion (and URL construction) happens BEFORE any of the 8 lines is printed: on an stoi failure stdout is byte-empty even with buffering disabled, so no partial record is ever emitted.",
    "evidence": "stdbuf -o0 with (\"http\",\"e.com\",\"BAD\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> exit=134, stdout_bytes=0; stdbuf -oL same input -> exit=134, stdout_bytes=0; piping the same input to od -c -> `0000000` (no bytes). Since per-line std::endl flushing is proven, zero bytes implies line 1 was never reached."
  },
  {
    "rule": "There is no option/flag parsing whatsoever. Arguments beginning with '-' or '--' (including \"--help\", \"-h\", \"--version\", bare \"-\") are treated as ordinary positional field values in every one of the 6 slots.",
    "evidence": "(\"--help\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> scheme \"--help\", to_string \"--help://example.com:80/\", exit=0. (\"-h\",...) -> scheme \"-h\", \"-h://example.com:80/\". (\"--version\",...) -> \"--version://example.com:80/\". (\"http\",\"--help\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> host \"--help\", \"http://--help:80/\". (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"--help\",\"\",\"\") -> path \"/--help\". (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"--help\",\"--help\") -> query \"--help\", fragment \"--help\", \"http://example.com:80/?--help#--help\". (\"-\",\"-\",\"80\",\"-\",\"-\",\"-\") -> \"-://-:80/-?-#-\", exit=0."
  },
  {
    "rule": "Argument bytes are passed through verbatim with no escaping, quoting, trimming, or sanitization — including spaces, tabs, other control bytes, and non-ASCII (non-UTF8-aware) bytes. Whitespace-only args are legitimate non-empty field values.",
    "evidence": "(\" \",\" \",\" 7 \",\" \",\" \",\" \") -> exit=0, lines 1,2 each a single space, line3 \"7\", line4 \"/ \", line7 \" :// :7/ ? # \", line8 1. (\"http\",\" \",\"80\",\" \",\" \",\" \") -> \"http:// :80/ ? # \". Tabs: (\"ht\\ttp\",\"ex\\tam\",\"80\",\"/a\\tb\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 \"ht\\ttp://ex\\tam:80/a\\tb\". Non-ASCII: scheme \"HTTP\\xc3\\x9c\" -> line1 od -c = `h t t p 303 234 \\n` (the 0xC3 0x9C bytes survive untouched, only ASCII letters case-folded). Huge args: 65536-char host and 100000-char path -> exit=0, stdout 331099 bytes, 8 lines, len(line2)=65537 incl. LF, len(line4)=100002."
  },
  {
    "rule": "Embedded newlines in arguments are NOT escaped, so the 8-record framing is purely positional and can be broken by callers: an arg containing '\\n' emits extra physical lines.",
    "evidence": "(\"ht\\ntp\",\"exa\\nmple.com\",\"80\",\"/a\\nb\",\"q\\n1\",\"f\\n2\") -> exit=0 but 18 physical lines: 1:ht 2:tp 3:exa 4:mple.com 5:80 6:/a 7:b 8:q 9:1 10:f 11:2 12:ht 13:tp://exa 14:mple.com:80/a 15:b?q 16:1#f 17:2 18:1. (\"a\\nb\",\"c\\nd\",\"80\",\"e\\nf\",\"g\\nh\",\"i\\nj\") -> stdout_lines=18, bytes=51. od of (\"ht\\ntp\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") shows `h t \\n t p \\n ...` — the LF is emitted raw."
  },
  {
    "rule": "No printf-style formatting is applied to arguments (output goes through ostream <<, not printf), so format specifiers and shell metacharacters are inert literals.",
    "evidence": "(\"%s%n%d\",\"%p%x\",\"80\",\"/%s\",\"%n\",\"%%\") -> exit=0, lines echo the literals and line7=\"%s%n%d://%p%x:80/%s?%n#%%\". (\"$(whoami)\",\"`id`\",\"80\",\"/;ls\",\"&&echo\",\"|cat\") -> line7=\"$(whoami)://`id`:80/;ls?&&echo#|cat\", exit=0, no command executed."
  },
  {
    "rule": "The program never reads stdin, and its behavior is independent of stdin state and of the environment.",
    "evidence": "Running with stdin redirected from a 2-line file then `cat`-ing the remainder printed \"REMAINING_STDIN:\" followed by both original lines (SHOULD_NOT_BE_CONSUMED, LINE2) — nothing consumed. With stdin closed (<&-): exit=0, stderr empty. `env -i` run of (\"http\",\"e.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> cksum \"4098084474 37\", exit=0 (same output as normal env). LC_ALL in {C, C.UTF-8, en_US.UTF-8, tr_TR.UTF-8, POSIX} for (\"HTTP\",\"H.IO\",\"80\",\"/PaTh\",\"\",\"\") all -> scheme=[http] host=[H.IO] path=[/PaTh] (no Turkish-I effect; C-locale ASCII case folding)."
  },
  {
    "rule": "Output is fully deterministic for a given argv, and stdout write errors are ignored (no error checking on the stream): a failed or closed stdout still yields exit 0 with empty stderr.",
    "evidence": "10 consecutive runs of (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") piped to cksum, `sort -u` collapsed to a single line \"3744289484 49\". stdout -> /dev/full: exit=0, stderr=[]. stdout closed (>&-): exit=0, stderr=[]."
  }
]

STATED UNCERTAINTIES (resolve these definitively by experiment):
[
  "A test15.cpp file exists next to the binary in /workspace/dataset/. Per the rules of engagement I deliberately did NOT read it; every rule above comes only from observed stdout/stderr/exit codes.",
  "IMPORTANT correction for other agents: a pre-existing helper at /tmp/p.sh silently pads/truncates to exactly 6 args (it invokes the binary as \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" \"$4\" \"$5\" \"$6\" regardless of $#), so any argc conclusions drawn with it are invalid — it makes argc<6 look like it succeeds. I used /tmp/q15.sh (true \"$@\" pass-through) instead. Verify your helper before trusting argc results.",
  "I could not directly count write() syscalls (no strace/ltrace available; gdb exists but using it would exceed the black-box rules). The per-line-flush conclusion is inferred from pipe read granularity across 300 runs: all 7 internal line boundaries were observed and no boundary ever fell mid-line. This is very strong but statistical rather than syscall-level proof.",
  "Because the stoi throw happens before any printing, I could not construct a case that emits some lines and then crashes, so I could not directly observe partial output surviving a crash.",
  "Whether the exit-1 path for argc<6 is an early `return 1` versus something that prints to a third stream is untestable beyond fd 1 and 2; both were confirmed byte-empty.",
  "The port normalization to -1 for values outside [0,65535] is observed behavior of the object, but I cannot tell from outside whether the clamp lives in the constructor/setter or in port() itself. Both produce identical observable output.",
  "Args containing a NUL byte cannot be passed via execve, so that case is untestable (as stated in the task).",
  "Whether the 8 lines come from 8 separate `std::cout << x << std::endl` statements or one chained expression with 8 endl inserts is indistinguishable from the outside; both yield the observed 8 line-aligned flushes.",
  "Line 1 lowercasing, line 4's leading-'/' insertion, and to_string()/is_valid() composition rules belong to other dimensions; I recorded them only incidentally and did not probe their boundaries."
]

